 No missing registry relaxes default behavior: the old out-of-scope diagnostic remains. No registry registration, state change, gate permission, review verdict, source edit or native decision is performed by these helpers. Root owns code integration, current module-closure resealing, actual drain/registration and normal native dispatch. Other finding classes and guards are unchanged.
 
 Focused tests are isolated fixtures, including the actual scope lifecycle. They cover native-output/prompt tampering, missing or forged identity, edited source or run marker, invented/duplicate homes, missing direct edges, changed original finding, unsupported verdicts, missing/stale seals, ordinary consumer amendment, and historical closure versus later source repair. Run the candidate test file under `node --test` from the repository root; it overlays candidate tools into disposable fixture trees and never installs canonical code or registries.
+
+## Native refuter extra read of the same registered direct context
+
+The existing registry also permits an actual refuter to open and flag that exact unchanged helper as **extra read-only supplier context**, while all required assigned coverage remains owed. No registry entry, subject scope or frontier ownership is added. Exactly one already registered reader context for the batch and helper ID must exist: a refuter schema flag has no consumer field, so multiple registered consumers/observations of that ID are ambiguous and reject. The matching reader obligation must actually be present in the batch's current version-2 scope, and its named direct consumer must remain assigned there. The entire existing reader/source/manifest authority is independently revalidated.
+
+For an extra opened registered helper, the actual original stable refuter result must be byte-identical to its immutable attempt result, successful in both process and terminal summary, have the exact role/label/single covered batch and prompt path, and contain a complete parsed structured tail equal to the entire canonical original refuter report. That report must actually open the helper once. The normalization binds its exact one-based flagged index and finding hash, canonical report raw hash, immutable attempt raw hash, prompt path/raw hash, original reader obligation and exact unchanged registered source hash. The prompt hash is a current collection-time binding; the successful result authenticates its native path, not an otherwise absent historical prompt-byte digest. Later changes invalidate the binding. No new historical refuter source-byte observation is invented: the source remains the exact unchanged reader-observed archive.
+
+Only such a registered helper can appear as an extra flagged context. Unrelated extra suppliers retain the prior rules: a generic read may be permitted as prerequisite context but does not acquire flagged scope; unrelated foreign flags, missing assigned coverage, invalid native output or arbitrary extra reads still reject. Normalized type is `unmanifested-draft-dependency`, with the existing reader binding plus `owner_unmanifested_refuter_context`. Its `observed_sha256` matches the exact registered reader carrier so independently adjudicated reader/refuter obligations can use the ordinary same-defect sharing rules. Its current decision carrier additionally seals the complete actual refuter observation binding. No producer batch is fabricated.
+
+Projection back to the original refuter schema strips every injected type, consumer, observed-source, authority and reader-anchor field. In particular it does **not** inject the reader's original `in-run-dependency` label into the original refuter flag, whose schema has no subject label. Both original reader and refuter obligations remain mandatory and distinct, as does every owned title flag. Alpha must independently adjudicate them all. The existing read-only verdict restriction, ordinary closed ledger, exact consumer and current seal apply to both. Sharing one nonfatal-recorded ledger row requires the existing explicit same-defect evidence and matching observed carrier; it does not discharge an omitted decision.
+
+The existing closure-evidence helper additionally freezes every collected refuter report, immutable attempt and prompt reference from these scope bindings. Active Step 3 revalidates complete native output, exact reader authority and unchanged source. After actual sealed closure, only historical references are verified; later legitimate source repairs remain allowed and do not claim new past reading. Original reports, source, registry and native decisions are never written by this extension. It does not request a fresh native reread for a legitimate already completed supplier-context read.
+
+Without any applicable registry context for the requested batch, refuter context discovery returns an empty map before applying version-2 anchor checks. Thus default legacy version-3 scopes and registries belonging only to other batches retain their previous behavior; an actual applicable opt-in still requires the strict version-2 reader scope.
